## 7. Classes

### 7.1 Visibility

Use TypeScript visibility modifiers (`private`, `protected`, `readonly`).
Never use ES private fields (`#field`). Omit `public` — it is the default and
is enforced by ESLint (`explicit-member-accessibility: no-public`).

```typescript
class TokenCache {
  private readonly tokens = new Map<string, Token>();
  private expiry: number;

  constructor(private readonly ttlMs: number) {
    this.expiry = 0;
  }

  get(key: string): Token | undefined {
    return this.tokens.get(key);
  }
}
```

### 7.2 Parameter Properties

Use parameter properties to reduce boilerplate in constructors.

```typescript
// Good.
class Client {
  constructor(
    private readonly host: string,
    private readonly token: string
  ) {}
}
```

### 7.3 readonly

Mark every property that is never reassigned after construction as `readonly`.

### 7.4 Getters and Setters

Getters must be pure (no side effects). Do not create pass-through accessors —
either make the property public/readonly or add real logic. Define accessors
directly on the class, never via `Object.defineProperty`.

### 7.5 Static Methods

Avoid private static methods; prefer module-level functions instead. Never use
`this` in a static method.

### 7.6 Constructors

Always include parentheses in constructor calls: `new Foo()`, not `new Foo`.

### 7.7 API Surface Minimization

Default to the smallest public API surface. Only export from `index.ts` what
consumers need. Internal types (options interfaces, schemas, helper functions)
must not be re-exported.

### 7.8 Field Initializers

Initialize fields where they are declared rather than in the constructor. This
reduces boilerplate and may let you drop the constructor entirely.

```typescript
// Good — initialized at declaration.
class TokenCache {
  private readonly entries = new Map<string, Token>();
  private lastRefresh = 0;
}

// Bad — initialization deferred to constructor.
class TokenCache {
  private readonly entries: Map<string, Token>;
  private lastRefresh: number;

  constructor() {
    this.entries = new Map();
    this.lastRefresh = 0;
  }
}
```

### 7.9 Arrow Function Properties

Do not use arrow functions as class properties. They create a new closure per
instance and obscure the fact that `this` is already bound, making the class
harder to reason about.

```typescript
// Bad — arrow property.
class Handler {
  private onClick = () => {
    this.handle();
  };
}

// Good — regular method with arrow at call site.
class Handler {
  private onClick(): void {
    this.handle();
  }

  register(el: Element): void {
    el.addEventListener('click', () => {
      this.onClick();
    });
  }
}
```

---

## 8. Control Flow

### 8.1 Braces

All control flow statements must use braced blocks, even for single statements.

```typescript
// Good.
if (token.isExpired()) {
  await refresh();
}

// Bad.
if (token.isExpired()) await refresh();
```

### 8.2 Equality

Always use `===` and `!==`. The only exception: `== null` is permitted to check
for both `null` and `undefined`.

```typescript
if (value === 0) { ... }
if (result == null) { ... } // Checks null and undefined.
```

### 8.3 Switch

Every switch must have a `default` case (even if empty). Non-empty case groups
must not fall through — terminate with `break`, `return`, or `throw`.

### 8.4 Iteration

Prefer `for...of` for arrays. Use `Object.entries()` for objects. Never use
`for...in` on arrays.

```typescript
for (const item of items) {
  process(item);
}

for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(config)) {
  applyOption(key, value);
}
```

### 8.5 Boolean Expressions

Strict boolean expressions are enforced by ESLint. Do not rely on implicit
coercion in conditionals for non-boolean values.

```typescript
// Good.
if (items.length > 0) { ... }

// Bad — implicit coercion of number to boolean.
if (items.length) { ... }
```

### 8.6 Assignment in Control Statements

Do not assign variables inside `if`, `while`, or other control-flow
conditions. Assignment is easily mistaken for an equality check.

```typescript
// Bad.
if (x = computeValue()) { ... }

// Good.
const x = computeValue();
if (x !== undefined) { ... }
```

---

## 9. Error Handling

### 9.1 Throw Only Errors

Always throw `Error` instances (or subclasses). Never throw strings, numbers,
or arbitrary objects — they lack stack traces.

```typescript
// Good.
throw new Error('Token refresh failed.');

// Bad.
throw 'Token refresh failed.';
```

### 9.2 Promise Rejection

Only reject promises with `Error` instances.

```typescript
Promise.reject(new Error('Request timed out.'));
```

### 9.3 Catch Clauses

Type caught values as `unknown` and narrow before use.

```typescript
try {
  await fetchToken();
} catch (e: unknown) {
  if (e instanceof Error) {
    log(e.message);
  }
  throw e;
}
```

### 9.4 Empty Catch Blocks

An empty catch block is only permitted with a comment explaining why the error
is intentionally swallowed.

```typescript
try {
  return parseJson(response);
} catch (_e: unknown) {
  // Response is not JSON. Fall through to text parsing.
}
```

### 9.5 Focused Try Blocks

Keep `try` blocks as small as possible. Only wrap the statement that may throw.
Process the result outside the `try` block to avoid accidentally swallowing
errors from unrelated code.

```typescript
// Good — only the throwing call is inside try.
let result: Response;
try {
  result = await client.send(request);
} catch (e: unknown) {
  throw new ApiError('Request failed.', {cause: e});
}
processResponse(result);

// Bad — processResponse errors are silently caught.
try {
  const result = await client.send(request);
  processResponse(result);
} catch (e: unknown) {
  throw new ApiError('Request failed.', {cause: e});
}
```

### 9.6 Result Types

For operations where failure is an expected, recoverable outcome (not an
exceptional condition), consider returning a discriminated union result type
instead of throwing. Reserve exceptions for truly unexpected failures.

```typescript
type ParseResult =
  | {ok: true; value: Token}
  | {ok: false; error: string};

function parseToken(raw: string): ParseResult {
  if (!raw.startsWith('dapi')) {
    return {ok: false, error: 'Token must start with "dapi".'};
  }
  return {ok: true, value: {accessToken: raw, tokenType: 'Bearer'}};
}
```

---

## 10. Comments and Documentation

### 10.1 Sentences

Every comment must be a proper sentence ending with a period.

### 10.2 JSDoc vs Line Comments

Use `/** JSDoc */` for documentation visible to consumers of the API (exported
functions, classes, interfaces). Use `//` comments for internal implementation
notes.

### 10.3 Multi-line Comments

Use multiple `//` lines, not `/* */` blocks.

```typescript
// Refresh the token if it expires within the next 30 seconds.
// This prevents race conditions when the token is used immediately
// after this check.
```

### 10.4 JSDoc Format

```typescript
/**
 * Refreshes the authentication token using the configured credentials.
 *
 * @param force - When true, refresh even if the token is not yet expired.
 * @returns The new token.
 * @throws {AuthError} If the credentials are invalid.
 */
function refreshToken(force?: boolean): Promise<Token> { ... }
```

Use Markdown in JSDoc bodies. Parameters use `@param name - Description`
format.

### 10.5 No Redundant Comments

Do not add comments that merely restate the code.

```typescript
// Bad — adds no information.
// Set count to zero.
const count = 0;
```

### 10.6 Parameter Name Comments

When a call site passes literal values whose meaning is not obvious from the
function name, use `/* paramName= */` comments. Before adding these, consider
whether the function should accept an options object instead.

```typescript
// Acceptable — clarifies ambiguous literals.
scheduleRetry(request, /* maxAttempts= */ 3, /* shouldLog= */ true);

// Better — options object eliminates the need for comments.
scheduleRetry(request, {maxAttempts: 3, shouldLog: true});
```

### 10.7 JSDoc Type Annotations Are Redundant

Do not duplicate type information in JSDoc that TypeScript already expresses.
Omit `@param` types, `@returns` types, `@implements`, `@enum`, `@private`, and
`@override` when the corresponding TypeScript syntax is present.

```typescript
// Good — description only, no redundant type.
/** @param force - Refresh even if the token is not yet expired. */
function refreshToken(force?: boolean): Promise<Token> { ... }

// Bad — duplicates the TypeScript type.
/** @param {boolean} force - Refresh even if the token is not yet expired. */
function refreshToken(force?: boolean): Promise<Token> { ... }
```

---

## 11. Formatting

Formatting is handled by **Prettier** and must not be overridden manually. The
canonical settings are:

| Setting          | Value      |
| ---------------- | ---------- |
| Semicolons       | Always     |
| Quotes           | Single     |
| Trailing commas  | ES5        |
| Bracket spacing  | None       |
| Print width      | 80         |
| Indent           | 2 spaces   |
| Tabs             | Never      |
| Arrow parens     | Avoid      |
| End of line      | LF         |

Run `npm run format` to auto-format. Run `npm run format:check` to verify.

---

## 12. Disallowed Features

The following are banned in this codebase:

| Feature                     | Reason                                                |
| --------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| `var`                       | Use `const` or `let`.                                 |
| `any`                       | Use `unknown` or a specific type.                     |
| `eval()` / `new Function()` | Security risk. No exceptions.                        |
| `with` statement            | Banned in strict mode.                                |
| `debugger`                  | Must never appear in committed code.                  |
| `console.*`                 | Use the `Logger` interface from `@databricks/sdk-databricks/logger`. Enforced by ESLint. |
| Default exports             | Use named exports only.                               |
| `const enum`                | Use plain `enum`.                                     |
| `@ts-ignore`                | Never suppress type errors. Fix the type instead.     |
| `@ts-expect-error`          | Same as above.                                        |
| `@ts-nocheck`               | Same as above.                                        |
| Wrapper objects             | Never use `new String()`, `new Boolean()`, `new Number()`. |
| `require()`                 | Use ES module `import` syntax.                        |
| ES private fields (`#`)     | Use TypeScript `private` modifier.                    |
| `for...in` on arrays        | Use `for...of` or array methods.                      |
| Non-null assertion (`!`)    | Use explicit checks. Enforced by ESLint.              |
| `namespace` keyword         | Use modules and separate files for namespacing.       |
| `{}` as a type              | Use `unknown`, `object`, or `Record<string, T>`.      |
| Standalone `_` identifier   | Use `_unused` prefix or elision in destructuring.     |
| Prototype manipulation      | Use `class` syntax. Never modify builtin prototypes.  |
| Unary `+` for coercion      | Use `Number()` to parse numeric values.               |

